  at a time, then approaches, then a design you approve section by section, then a written spec. The ratchet is one-way,
  so I won't quietly drop back down.

  Recording what I've got so far: a persistent identity that outlives a single [38;5;153mlogin()[39m call, readable from anywhere in
  the app, and consumable by forms that don't exist yet.

  First question, and it shapes everything after it. Right now [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is synchronous and fabricates its own result — it
  never touches [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m. Whether a real server is in play decides the shape of the whole thing:

  - [1mReal backend now[0m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m becomes async and returns a Promise; the [38;5;153muserId[39m is whatever the server says it is, and
    the store holds a server-issued identity. Every current and future caller has to [38;5;153mawait[39m. That's the change that's
    expensive to reverse, because it rewrites the signature for all those later forms too.
  - [1mStub for now, real backend later[0m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m stays synchronous and mints a placeholder [38;5;153muserId[39m; the store and its
    interface get built and tested today. Cheap now, but you pay the async conversion later, and at that point the later
    forms exist and all have to change with it.
  - [1mStub, but async-shaped from day one[0m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m returns a Promise that currently resolves a fake [38;5;153muserId[39m. Slightly
    awkward to read while it's fake, but the signature never changes again.

  [1mMy recommendation: the third.[0m The async boundary is the one decision here that gets more expensive the longer it
  waits, and you've just told me more callers are coming. Paying for it while there's exactly one call site is the
  cheapest it will ever be.
